==English==
{{was wotd|2006|August|29}}

===Pronunciation===
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-make a mountain out of a molehill.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-make a mountain out of a molehill.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|[[make]]<,,made> [[a]] [[mountain]] [[out of]] [[a]] [[molehill]]}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[treat#Verb|treat]] a [[problem]] as [[greater]] than it is; to [[blow out of proportion|blow something out of proportion]]; to [[exaggerate]] the [[importance]] of something [[trivial]].
#: {{ux|en|If you’re stuck in traffic, try not to '''make a mountain out of a molehill''' worrying about it too much. You could be making it much worse.}}
#* '''1573''', {{w|John Bridges (bishop)|John Bridges}}, ''The Supremacie of Christian Princes Over All Persons Throughout Theor Dominions,'' London: Humphrey Toye, Division{{nbsp}}18, p.{{nbsp}}553,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A16835.0001.001]</sup>
#*: to slaunder the Gospell ye aggrauate many lies, & will '''make a mountaine of a mole hill'''
#* '''1642''', {{w|James Howell}}, ''Instructions for Forreine Travell,'' London: Humphrey Mosley, Section{{nbsp}}14, p.{{nbsp}}176-177,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A44724.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Others have a custome to bee always relating strange things and wonders, {{...}} they '''make Mountaines of Mole-hils''', like Charenton-Bridge-Eccho, which doubles the sound nine times.
#* '''1749''', {{w|Henry Fielding}}, ''{{w|The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling}},'' Dublin, John Smith, Volume{{nbsp}}3, Book{{nbsp}}15, Chapter{{nbsp}}10, pp.{{nbsp}}170-171,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004794856.0001.003]</sup>
#*: For of all the Powers exercised by this Passion over our Minds, one of the most wonderful is that of supporting Hope in the midst of Despair. {{...}} Yet it is equally true, that the same Passion will sometimes '''make Mountans of Mole-hills''', and produce Despair in the midst of Hope;
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1868|author={{w|Wilkie Collins}}|title={{w|The Moonstone}}|location=London|publisher=Tinsley Brothers|volume=1|chapter=12|page=207|url=https://archive.org/details/moonstoneromance01coll/page/207/mode/1up?q=%22making+a+mountain+out+of+a+molehill%22
|passage=“I have now only one remark to offer, on leaving this case in your hands. There ''is'' such a thing, Sergeant, as '''making a mountain out of a molehill'''. Good morning.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author={{w|D. H. Lawrence}}|title={{w|Sons and Lovers}}|location=London|publisher=Heinemann|section=Part 2, Chapter 7, p. 181|url=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.23975/page/n187/mode/1up?q=%22made+such+a+mountain+of+this+molehill%22
|passage=She wondered why he, who did not usually trouble over trifles, '''made such a mountain of this molehill'''.}}

